    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044463": {
      "title": "MAP Student Advisor, 0.4FTE, Specified Purpose, Maynooth University Access Programme (MAP)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Job Title : MAP Student Advisor Department : Access Office Vacancy ID : 044463 Closing Date : 01-Nov-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. Our greatest impact on society is through inspiring and educating creative, inquisitive and critically engaged minds. We have a reputation for authentic, strengths-based access initiatives, supporting participation, engagement, and progression for all students. We recognise that individual student needs vary greatly and that in addition to our universal student supports, dedicated support for complex additional needs is hugely beneficial in ensuring student success. Our Maynooth Access Programme (MAP) Student Advisors are a key part of how we support the access, retention, and success of students from groups identified in the National Access Plan 2022 -2028 (NAP) as having low participation in higher education. These groups include students from socio-economically disadvantaged backgrounds, mature students, further education award holders, lone parents, refugees and protection applicants, care-experienced students, students with disabilities, and members of the Traveller and Roma communities. MAP Student Advisors provide tailored, student-centred guidance and support through both one-to-one and group settings. They assist students identified in the NAP as having low participation in higher education in navigating university life and conduct individual needs assessments to identify additional supports as needed, to help students thrive. The MAP Student Advisor role involves assessing, coordinating, and ensuring integrated delivery of a broad range of internal and external academic, personal and financial supports, for students from NAP priority groups. MAP Student Advisors work collaboratively with colleagues across the University and with external stakeholders. This post is a MAP Student Advisor role with a particular focus on assistive technology within the wider student advisory service. Reporting to the MAP Student Advisory Manager within the Maynooth Access Programme (MAP), MAP Student Advisors are based in the Maynooth Access Office as part of a multidisciplinary team that includes outreach and student support professionals, clinical and technical specialists, and administrative staff. While MAP Student Advisors currently report to the MAP Student Advisory Manager, the breadth of engagement with colleagues across the university means that reporting lines can change in line with future organisational/structural reviews and developments as the service expands and develops. Administrative Officer II (2026) Salary Scale: €47,387 – €67,129 p.a. pro-rata 0.4FTE (9 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 1st of November 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044223": {
      "title": "Pastoral Care Advisor (AOII), Specified Purpose, Student Services",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Job Title : Pastoral Care Advisor Department : Student Services Vacancy ID : 044223 Closing Date : 11-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. We are seeking an enthusiastic and committed Pastoral Care Advisor to join our Student Services team. The role of the Pastoral Care Advisor will be to support students through the provision of holistic care and organisation of faith-based activities alongside the other advisory and central student services. The Pastoral Care Advisor will work as part of the Student Support Division of Student Services, within the newly developed Office for Students & Learning, and will work within a wider team who offer support to a wonderfully diverse body of students, registered at both Maynooth University and St Patrick's Pontifical University. Applicants for the post should ideally have experience working in Pastoral Care, or a similar role, preferably within a Higher Education setting. In addition to their pastoral care skills, the successful candidate should be able to demonstrate excellent organisational, people and communication skills, with a proven ability to work well in a team environment, while also demonstrating their ability to use initiative and be a self-starter. This is a unique role within the area of student support and the candidate should be experienced in dealing with varied and complex situations, while demonstrating confidentiality and sensitivity in all interactions with students, our staff, and the public. As this position operates within Student Services, candidates should understand and have empathy with the mission, ethos, and overall approach of Student Services. The successful candidate will report to the Student Support Officer, who in turn reports to the Director of Student Services. Salary: Administrative Officer II (2026): €47,387 – €67,129 p.a. (9 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 11th of October 2026. Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044543": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher (Specified Purpose contract), National Centre for Inclusive Higher Education",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Department : National Centre for Inclusive Higher Edu Vacancy ID : 044543 Closing Date : 25-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research, scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher with a strong passion for inclusive education. The successful candidate will contribute to cutting-edge research projects aimed at understanding and addressing barriers to inclusion in higher education. This role requires a blend of excellent quantitative and qualitative research skills, and a proven track record of publications is highly desirable. Post Doctoral Researcher Level 1 (2026) Salary Scale: €47, 273 - €49, 890) pa. (3 Points) Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 25th October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044523": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher (Specified Purpose Contract)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Department : Design Innovation Vacancy ID : 044523 Closing Date : 25-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research, scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. Our Health and Wellbeing research beacon focuses on enhancing quality of life, advancing healthcare innovations, and addressing the root causes of disease.We are seeking to appoint a Post-Doctoral Researcher to join the Maynooth University ‘ Design of an Epidermolysis Bullosa Simplex inSole’ (DEBSS) team for a two-year research position. Funded by Debra Research, and in collaboration with the University of Bristol, the DEBSS project aims to develop and validate a cooling insole for people with EBS. Post Doctoral Researcher Level 1 (2026) Salary Scale: €47,273 (1 point) Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 25th October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044183": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Specified Purpose Contract, Department of Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Department : Biology Vacancy ID : 044183 Closing Date : 14-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Research Ireland funded “Future Forests” project. The Project Increasing tree diversity in planted forests can enhance ecosystem services such as wood production and carbon sequestration. Ambitious afforestation plans form a major part of Irelands climate mitigation strategy. Diversification of Irish forests is also a key component of national forest policy. However, we know little about how mixed forests will function under future climates. In a novel field-based open-top chamber experiment, the Future Forests project is assessing the performance of tree seedling monocultures and mixtures under elevated CO2. This experiment provides a platform for testing mechanisms controlling responses of sapling monocultures and mixtures to climate change e.g., altered phenology, physiological limitations to establishment/productivity. In addition, the project is using long-term forest inventory data from European temperate forests to assess the relationship between tree diversity, growth, and mortality of trees under global change. The Role Increasing tree diversity in planted forests can enhance ecosystem services such as wood production and carbon sequestration. Ambitious afforestation plans form a major part of Irelands climate mitigation strategy. Diversification of Irish forests is also a key component of national forest policy. However, we know little about how mixed forests will function under future climates. In a novel field-based open-top chamber experiment, the Future Forests project is assessing the performance of tree seedling monocultures and mixtures under elevated CO2. This experiment provides a platform for testing mechanisms controlling responses of sapling monocultures and mixtures to climate change e.g., altered phenology, physiological limitations to establishment/productivity. In addition, the project is using long-term forest inventory data from European temperate forests to assess the relationship between tree diversity, growth, and mortality of trees under global change. The researcher will play a leading role in assessing above- and below-ground physiological responses of saplings grown in monoculture and mixtures to elevated CO2. Measurements will include leaf gas analysis, chlorophyll fluorescence, leaf traits, and sapling biomass. The researcher will also contribute to an analysis of long-term inventory data from European forests to assess the relationship between diversity, growth, and mortality of trees under the environmental change (elevated CO2, temperature, drought) of the last 40 years. The researcher will contribute to the wider project and work with collaborators at University College Dublin, Trinity College Dublin, the Smithsonian Institution, and Kyoto University. Salary: Post-Doctoral Researcher (Level 1): €47, 273 – €53,925 p.a. (1-6 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Wednesday, 14th of October. Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044303": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Specified Purpose, Department of Biology, Maynooth University",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Department : Biology Vacancy ID : 044303 Closing Date : 01-Nov-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. We are seeking an excellent and highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to work with Dr Martina Schroeder’s research group at Maynooth University and her national and international collaborators on a HRB-funded project that aims to generate a hiPSC-based preclinical model for DDX3X syndrome, a rare neurodevelopmental disease caused by germline mutations in the RNA helicase and innate immune signalling adaptor molecule DDX3X The Project The project will then use the new cellular model to analyse effects of DDX3X syndrome mutations on macrophage differentiation and function, with a focus on NLRP3 inflammasome activation. This is based on published work that characterised DDX3X as a positive regulator of NLRP3. This project will therefore explore whether DDX3X syndrome associated mutations may cause issues with NLRP3 activation and innate immunity in patients with DDX3X mutations, something that is difficult to ascertain from clinical data due to the complex mutational landscape and the rarity of the condition, and thus the project findings have direct relevance for patients. The project is highly collaborative (involving researchers at UoG, QUB and UCSC) and incorporates public-patient involvement (PPI), thus there will be many opportunities for the postdoctoral researcher to expand their professional network and skill set. We are therefore seeking a researcher who is comfortable working with multiple collaborators and engaging with patient groups and other stakeholders. There will also be opportunities to attend and present at both national and international conferences, and for continued professional development Salary: Post-Doctoral Researcher (Level 1): €47, 273 (point 1 with increments) Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 1st of November 2026. Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=043683": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Specified Purpose, Department of History",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Department : School of History & Ancient Classics Vacancy ID : 043683 Closing Date : 11-Oct-2026 We are seeking an excellent scholar to join a project team led by Prof. Raul Cârstocea, who has been awarded a European Research Council (ERC) Consolidator Grant for a project entitled Insurgent Temporalities: Fascism as a Global Anti-Universalist Project (INTEMPO). The postdoctoral researcher will be part of a team undertaking a novel analysis of fascism during the interwar period and the Second World War, understood as a global phenomenon. They will engage in archival research, co-organise the activities of the project and contribute to the publications ensuing from them, and co-lead with the PI one of the work packages of the project, as detailed below. They will have the opportunity to develop their research, both individually and as part of a team of ambitious scholars. Postdoctoral Researcher (2026) Salary scale: €47,273 – €47,880p.a. (2 points) Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 11th October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=043903": {
      "title": "Professor (B) of Health Law (Permanent), School of Law and Criminology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Job Title : Professor (B) of Health Law Department : School of Law & Criminology Vacancy ID : 043903 Closing Date : 18-Oct-2026 Maynooth University School of Law and Criminology seeks an outstanding academic to join our staff as Professor of Health Law. This new Professor post is part of the university commitment to the Health and Wellbeing and Society and Public Policy research beacons of our Strategic Plan 2023-2028. The appointee will contribute research-informed, practice-engaged education, scholarship, and societal impact that demonstrates Maynooth University’s impact in supporting research-informed public policy in Ireland and Europe. We have a particular interest in applicants with expertise in legal research, scholarship, and teaching as they relate to one or more of the following areas of health law, amongst other areas of health law: Health law and its intersection with new and emerging technologies, including but not limited to, for example: assistive reproductive technologies, emerging biotechnologies, digital technologies, artificial intelligence; The private and commercial law aspects of health law (e.g. family law related to healthcare; tort law related to healthcare; private ordering and contract law within healthcare contexts; health insurance; healthcare financing; legal frameworks related to translational research in healthcare markets, etc); Competition and state aid law and the provision of healthcare; Pharmaceutical regulation; Regulatory frameworks for healthcare provision; Healthcare resource allocation; Health and criminal justice. Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary university goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. The appointee to this role will be expected to provide significant intellectual leadership, through a demonstrated commitment to both education and research, and to contribute towards the effective leadership and management of the School, the Faculty, and the University. The appointee will therefore provide effective leadership in legal research and education, both through their own work and through mentorship of other faculty and research students. The new Professor of Health Law will also work with the Head of School, with other Professors, and with Faculty and Staff of the School of Law and Criminology to support the development and operation of the School’s wider research, education and service agenda. The ideal appointee will have an excellent track record in high-impact research, scholarship, and publication, in attracting external research funding, in leading research teams, in teaching and educational innovation, in the development and delivery of academic programmes, in service both to academic life and to society, and in academic leadership. The appointee will be expected to sustain and conduct research of the highest impact and standard, to engage in scholarship of quality and substance, and to generate publications of international standard. The appointee will be expected to continue to build a strong research profile that supports the University’s research strategy, including affiliating to its research institutes, where appropriate, and to one or more of the School’s, Faculty’s or University’s research centres or institutes, and working with colleagues on national and international research. The new Professor (B) of Health Law will be expected to contribute significantly to the research income-raising capacity of the school. This will be through their own funding applications, supporting the external research fund-raising capacity and enablement of the School and Faculty, and in cooperation with departments and schools across the University. The appointee will be expected to make a strong contribution to the teaching programme of the School and to undertake teaching duties on the School’s undergraduate and postgraduate programmes as well as the supervision of postgraduate and postdoctoral research students/fellows. Please note that, as part of the workload allocation process, teaching duties are assigned by the Head of the School, their delegate or the Dean of the Faculty of Social Sciences. This Professorship forms part of a coordinated Faculty-level investment in Health and Wellbeing across the Faculty of Social Sciences at Maynooth University. Through a cluster of permanent Professor (B) appointments, the University aims to strengthen interdisciplinary leadership, enhance research-informed and practice-engaged education, and deliver meaningful societal and policy impact in Ireland and internationally. While each role is embedded within its home School or Department, the postholder will be expected to collaborate across the Faculty and University, contributing to collective research, teaching, and engagement activities that maximise impact in the Health and Wellbeing domain. Salary: Professor B (2026) €106,610 - €142,500 (6 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 18th October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044443": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, 0.3FTE, Specified Purpose, Department of Media Studies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Job Title : Research Assistant Department : Media Studies Vacancy ID : 044443 Closing Date : 18-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. The research project Screening Mobility is now seeking to appoint a Research Assistant. The Research Assistant will support the project team with the logistics and administration associated with organising the networks’ online and in person meetings. Screening Mobility studies migration and mobility in Ireland and the UK’s Cultural and Creative industries (CCIs). Screen Industries are a priority sector in the UK’s Creative Industries Sector Plan and have central status in Ireland's Digital Creative Industries Roadmap. They make a major contribution to the economic output of CCIs. Like many other CCIs, Screen Industries face challenges of sustaining career trajectories; precarious working conditions; gender, racial, and other demographic inequalities; skills shortages; and increased technological and global competition. Screen Industries particular reliance on migration and mobility (through organisations’ and individuals’ short-, long-term, or permanent movement to facilitate production) creates both opportunities and concerns for responding to these challenges. These opportunities and concerns raise complex questions around industry and livelihood sustainability as well as access, belonging, and cultural differences across local, national, and international scales. However, migration and mobility remain under-researched when considering the economic, social and cultural importance of Screen Industries and CCIs more broadly. The objective of Screening Mobility is to create a new network of policy, industry and academic experts on migration and mobility in the UK and Ireland's Screen Industries to address these opportunities and challenges. It will consider new perspectives and solutions regarding the structural forces that shape opportunity, constraint, and precarity in both Screen Industries and the wider CCIs. Working under agreed, shared principles, it will bring together a ‘triple helix’ of academics, industry and policymakers. This collaboration will develop knowledge on the extent of mobility in Screen Industries, as well as the experiences and facilitators of, and barriers to, mobility and migration. Sharing policy, industry and research expertise will augment the development of evidence, which would not be possible were the range of stakeholders involved to remain working in their geographical, organisational and disciplinary silos. The objective will be achieved through a variety of activities focused on co-producing answers to 4 research questions: 1. What data and what sources are needed to best understand migration and mobility in the UK and Ireland’s Screen Industries? 2. What are the experiences of migration and mobility in the UK and Ireland’s Screen Industries? 3. What are the regulatory regimes of migration and mobility in Screen Industries in the UK and Ireland? 4. What are the economic, social and cultural impacts of migration and mobility in the UK and Ireland’s Screen Industries? Screening Mobility will co-produce answers to these research questions by fusing research excellence from across the team through several different kinds of activities. Online and in-person workshops, talks, events and creative outputs will connect qualitative and quantitative analysis with creative and participatory research practice. The activities will share data and methods, and will create new shared languages for academic, policy and industry voices. Salary Scale : Research Assistant (Level 1): €34,130 – €37,173 pro rata (0.3FTE) p.a. (5 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 18th October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044323": {
      "title": "Senior Undergraduate Student Recruitment Manager, Permanent, Admissions & Student Recruitment Office",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Job Title : Snr. Student Recruitment Mgr (Undergrad) Department : Admissions Office Vacancy ID : 044323 Closing Date : 26-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which excellent research and outstanding education remain deeply interconnected and equally valued. With students at the centre of this mission, undergraduate student recruitment plays a critical role in supporting informed student choice, widening participation and sustainable institutional growth. Maynooth University is seeking an experienced and strategic Senior Undergraduate Student Recruitment Manager – Undergraduate (EU) to lead and develop the University’s Undergraduate Student Recruitment team and contribute to the development and delivery of its undergraduate CAO recruitment strategy. Reporting to the Director of Admissions, the postholder will provide strategic and operational leadership for undergraduate student recruitment activity, supporting growth in CAO applications while ensuring a high-quality, student-centred experience throughout the recruitment journey. The postholder will lead a high-performing team responsible for outreach, engagement and relationship management across the second-level and Further Education sectors, working with schools, Guidance Counsellors, FE providers, parents/guardians and other key influencers in the student decision-making process. The role will oversee a comprehensive programme of student recruitment activity, including school and FE engagement, Open Days, campus visits, exhibitions, information sessions, Guidance Counsellor engagement and digital recruitment. The postholder will ensure that activity is effectively targeted, evidence-based and aligned with University priorities and agreed recruitment objectives. The Senior Undergraduate Student Recruitment Manager will also oversee undergraduate student recruitment communications and publications, including the undergraduate prospectus, supporting print and digital materials and CRM-led communications with prospective students, parents/guardians, teachers and Guidance Counsellors. A key responsibility will be the analysis of recruitment performance, applicant behaviour, CAO trends and wider market intelligence. The postholder will provide clear, timely and evidence-based advice to the Director of Admissions and relevant University stakeholders, identifying opportunities and risks relating to application growth, conversion, diversification, programme demand and the changing undergraduate student recruitment environment. The postholder will work closely with Communications and Marketing, academic departments, Admissions and professional service teams to ensure coordinated student recruitment activity, consistent messaging and an excellent prospective student experience. They will also represent Maynooth University externally and develop strong strategic relationships across the second-level and Further Education sectors and relevant professional networks. The role requires flexibility to work outside normal office hours, including evenings and weekends, particularly during key recruitment periods and major events, and to travel nationally as required. Salary: Senior Administrative Officer IV (2026): €71,441 – €113,437 p.a. (1-7 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Monday, 26th of October 2026. Please note all applications must be made via our Online Recruitment Portal at the following link: https://www.maynoothuniversity.ie/human-resources/vacancies Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=044563": {
      "title": "Technical Officer, Permanent, Electronic Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Job Title : Technical Officer Department : Electronic Engineering Vacancy ID : 044563 Closing Date : 08-Nov-2026 The Role This post is to facilitate teaching, learning and research within the Department of Electronic Engineering. The Technical Officer will play a key role in ensuring the effective and efficient delivery of undergraduate teaching, experimental project work (capstone, MEng and MSc level) and research in the department. They will maintain and repair equipment to ensure continuity of teaching and research in the Department. They will provide hands-on training and demonstrate standard operating procedures to all students and teaching assistants in the use of a variety of experimental equipment based in the department. This role will also provide advice and occasional support for the staff in the Maynooth International Engineering College. Technical Officer (2026): €48,095 – €62,938 p.a. (7 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 8th of November 2026. Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuimrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=043943": {
      "title": "University Tutor in Criminology, Permanent, School of Law & Criminology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Job Title : University Tutor in Criminology Department : School of Law & Criminology Vacancy ID : 043943 Closing Date : 11-Oct-2026 Maynooth University is committed to a strategy in which the primary University goals of excellent research and scholarship and outstanding education are interlinked and equally valued. The University Tutor plays an important role in the delivery of many of the University’s academic programmes and is appointed for the primary purpose of contributing to teaching within a clear and established teaching programme by providing tuition and/or demonstrations to groups of, usually undergraduate students, in specific aspects of the programme. The University Tutor assists and supports students to further develop their understanding of key course topics and to draw out key learning points from course materials. University Tutor Scale (2026) Salary Scale: €46,007– €58,500 p.a. (7 points) Appointments will be made in accordance with public sector pay provisions. Closing Date: 23:30hrs (local Irish time) on Sunday, 11 October 2026 Applications must be submitted by the closing date and time specified above. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late applications will not be accepted. Maynooth University is an equal opportunities employer The position is subject to the Statutes of the University __________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Tá Ollscoil Mhá Nuad tiomanta do straitéis ina ndéantar príomhspriocanna na hOllscoile maidir le taighde agus scoláireacht den scoth agus oideachas sármhaith a idirnascadh agus ina léirítear an meas céanna ar an dá ghné. Tá ról tábhachtach ag an Teagascóir Ollscoile i seachadadh go leor de chláir acadúla na hOllscoile agus ceaptar iad go príomha chun rannchuidiú leis an teagasc laistigh de chlár teagaisc atá soiléir agus seanbhunaithe, trí theagasc agus/nó taispeántais a chur ar fáil do ghrúpaí mac léinn, mic léinn fochéime de ghnáth, maidir le gnéithe sonracha den chlár. Cabhraíonn an Teagascóir Ollscoile le mic léinn agus tacaíonn siad leo chun a dtuiscint ar phríomhábhair an chúrsa a fhorbairt a thuilleadh agus chun na príomhphointí foghlama a bhaint as ábhair an chúrsa. Tuarastal Teagascóir Ollscoile (2026) : €46,007– €58,500 p.a. (7 bpointe) De réir fhorálacha pá na hearnála poiblí a dhéanfar ceapacháin. Dáta Deiridh: 23:30 (am na hÉireann) ar an Domhnach, 11 Deireadh Fómhair 2026 Caithfear iarratais a chur isteach faoin dáta deiridh agus faoin am a shonraítear thuas. Cealóidh an córas go huathoibríoch iarratais atá fós idir lámha ag an am deiridh ar an dáta deiridh sonraithe. Ní ghlacfar le hiarratais mhalla. Is fostóir comhdheiseanna í Ollscoil Mhá Nuad Tá an post seo faoi réir Reachtanna na hOllscoile"
    }
